# How Godot's VoxelGI Lights a Room {#video subtitle="Baked voxel global illumination in Godot 4.7, followed through the source at the engine's sizes" engineVersion=4.7.2-stable commit=ed1daf0bf001b61586d9930840f2f1394092c079 date=2026-10-31 fps=30 width=1920 height=1080}

## Where VoxelGI runs {#c1 number=1 target=305 summary="What VoxelGI adds, the problem it solves, the parts it is made of, and what decides whether it runs." sources="frame-order, voxelgi-gate, voxelgi-doc, mobile-stub, gles3-stub, node-defaults, quality-setting"}

### VoxelGI adds the light that bounced off the room {#c1-room kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {voxelgiMix 0.0}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 18.0}
    {cameraDistance 6.4}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 14.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 26.0 20.0 5.2} {pose 20.0 17.0 6.3}}}}}}}

This is the lab's study room, lit only by its ceiling panel and the sky.

A surface the panel cannot reach directly gets only the sky's flat, even light.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Now Godot's VoxelGI adds the light that bounces around the room"}.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The red wall warms the sphere and the floor beside it, and the corners fill in"}.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The light that reaches a surface after bouncing comes from every other surface it can see"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 1.0 0.3}}
    "Gathering that light ray by ray, for every pixel in every frame, would cost far too much"}.

${cue "key" {' {cellView 0.0} {cutHeight 1.6}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "So VoxelGI cuts the room into a grid of small boxes, called cells, ahead of time"}.

${cue "key" {' {cellView 4.0}}}${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.3 1.0}}
    "It then lights those cells"}.

Every pixel then reads that light back through a few cones, shapes that widen as they go.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 2.0}}
    "One sample of a cone averages many cells at once, so a pixel needs only a few samples"}.

### Baked once {#c1-map-bake kind=2d density=0.4 graph=frame focus="BAKE, SDF"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "bake-entry"}}}
That ahead-of-time step is called the bake.

It usually runs once, in Godot's editor.

VoxelGI covers only the part of the room inside its box.

The bake turns the meshes inside that box into a tree of cells, in which each cell splits into eight smaller ones.

This tree is called an octree.

### And a distance field {#c1-map-sdf kind=2d density=0.4 graph=frame focus="SDF"}

The bake also makes a distance field, which records how far each place is from the nearest cell that holds a surface.

Later, that field helps find which cells a shadow-casting light cannot reach, so those cells get none of its light.

### Lit when something changes {#c1-map-light kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="LIGHT, TEX, DYN"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "relight-gate"}}}
At run time, compute passes light the smallest cells, those at the bottom of the octree.

They store the result in a three-dimensional texture.

These compute passes are called the light passes.

The light passes run again only when a light, the VoxelGI's data, or one of a few of its settings changes.

So in a still room, they cost nothing.

### Moving objects, every frame {#c1-map-dynamic kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="DYN"}

An object meant to move is marked Dynamic, and the bake leaves it out.

Instead, it is drawn into the light passes' texture every frame.

### Read every frame {#c1-map-read kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="PRE, CONE"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "process-gi"}}}
Every frame, a compute pass called the screen pass traces cones through the light passes' texture.

It traces them for each pixel whose surface lies inside the VoxelGI's box.

### Into the image {#c1-map-opaque kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="OPAQUE"}

${cue "key" {' {voxelgiMix 0.0}}}
Then comes the opaque pass, which shades the scene's solid surfaces.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "It mixes the cones' light into the image as those surfaces' indirect light"}.

By the end, you will be able to follow one pixel's light back through each of these parts.

### The parts, on the room {#c1-parts kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoBounds panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {showBox 1.0}
