${over
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 7.0}}
    {overText
        {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 1.4}}
        "Here are the room's baked cells, drawn as one point each"}}.

${over {' {cellViewRange 0.0 4.0}} {overText {' {cutHeightRange 1.4 2.4}} "Each cell stores light"}}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 5.0}}
    "Each bigger cell up the octree stores a blend of the eight smaller cells inside it"}.

${cue "key" {'
    demoPixel
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {coneSteps 16.0}
    {specCone 0.0}
    panelHidden}}${overText
        {' {conesShownRange 0.0 4.0}}
        "Here, one pixel reads that light through its few cones"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 1.0 16.0}}
    "As a cone widens, it reads bigger cells, like a texture read at a coarser mip"}.

### Forward+ only, on whenever a VoxelGI is in view {#c1-gate kind=3d density=0.4}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelValues 1.0 {' {focusRow 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 16.0 18.0} {aim 8.0 22.0} {aim 14.0 19.0}}}}}}}

Godot draws each frame with one of its three renderers.

${cue "key" {' {callout "voxelgi-doc"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "VoxelGI works only in the one called Forward+"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "node-defaults"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 4.0}}
    "A VoxelGI is a node placed in the scene"}.

${overText {' {showBoxRange 0.0 1.0}} "By default it is a box twenty metres on each side"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "gles3-stub"}}}${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "Of the other two renderers, Compatibility leaves every VoxelGI function empty, so it does nothing for VoxelGI"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "mobile-stub"}}}The Mobile renderer never records which VoxelGI box each object lies in, so no surface there reads VoxelGI's light.

No project setting turns VoxelGI on or off.

${cue "key" {' {callout "voxelgi-gate"}}}${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Instead, its work runs in any frame where culling, the test of what is in view, finds a VoxelGI"}.

${cue "key" {' {cutHeight 1.6} {cellView 0.0}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 4.0 7.0}}
    "By default, the node's box is cut into one hundred twenty-eight cells along its longest axis"}.

So the default twenty-metre box gets cells about sixteen centimetres wide.

No project setting turns VoxelGI on, but one does change its result.

${cue "key" {' {callout "quality-setting"}}}${over
    {' {focusRowRange 4.0 12.0}}
    {overText
        {' {qualityRange 0.0 1.0}}
        "It picks how many cones each pixel traces for diffuse light, four by default, or six for a finer result"}}.

The other settings come up in the chapters that use them.

### The lab's box: six point four metres, five-centimetre cells {#c1-box kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoBounds panelValues 1.0 {' {showBox 0.0} {boundsStep 0.0} {focusRow -1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 22.0 30.0 12.0} {pose 38.0 36.0 10.5}}}}}}}

${over
    {' {showBoxRange 0.0 1.0}}
    {overText
        {' {focusRowRange -1.0 0.0}}
        "The lab places one VoxelGI around its room, six point four by three point two by six point four metres"}}.

${over
    {' {focusRowRange 0.0 4.0}}
    {overText
        {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 7.0}}
        "With the default one hundred twenty-eight cells along its longest side, its finest cells are five centimetres wide"}}.

${over
    {' {focusRowRange 4.0 6.0}}
    {overText
        {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 1.0}}
        "A full grid of them would hold about a million cells, but the next chapter shows that the bake makes far fewer"}}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 -1.0}}
    "Like Godot's editor, the lab bakes this box on the processor, with code ported from Godot's own"}.

${cue "key" {' {cellView 4.0}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 7.0}}
    "The lab lights the cells and reads them back on the graphics card, as Godot does at run time"}.

A copy of that run-time code on the processor checks that the two results agree.

### Next: the bake {#c1-recap kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {voxelgiMix 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cellView 4.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 18.0 6.4} {pose 32.0 22.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${overText {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 7.0}} "So VoxelGI is a box of baked cells"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The cells are lit when the lights change, and every pixel inside the box reads them back"}.

Everything VoxelGI can light starts in the bake, so the next chapter follows the bake.

### Where to read {#c1-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="frame-order, voxelgi-gate, voxelgi-doc, mobile-stub, gles3-stub, node-defaults, quality-setting"}

These are the lines this chapter followed, from the frame's VoxelGI steps to the node's defaults.

