When the video is built, a check confirms that the Godot source is still at the commit those lines were read from.

## Baking the octree {#c2 number=2 target=355 summary="The bake makes the box a cube of cells, sends every triangle down an octree, and samples it in each leaf it reaches." sources="bake-entry, find-meshes, begin-bake, plot-mesh, plot-face-recurse, tri-box, leaf-scan, material-cache"}

### The bake comes first {#c2-map kind=2d density=0.2 graph=frame focus=BAKE}

Everything VoxelGI knows about the room comes from one bake, so this chapter starts there.

### Static meshes inside the box are baked {#c2-collect kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoBounds panelValues 1.0 {'
    {showBox 1.0}
    {boundsStep 0.0}
    {focusRow -1.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cellView 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 32.0 11.0} {pose 44.0 34.0 10.0} {pose 36.0 30.0 10.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "bake-entry"}}}${overText
    {' {showBoxRange 0.4 1.0}}
    "Pressing Bake starts the voxelizer, the code that turns the meshes in the VoxelGI's box into cells"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "find-meshes"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 3.0}}
    "The voxelizer first walks the scene, starting at the VoxelGI's parent node, and collects the meshes to bake"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "A mesh is collected when it is visible and its bounds touch the box"}.

Each object also has a GI mode.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 6.0}}
    "A collected mesh is baked only when that mode is Static, the default"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 6.0 7.0}}
    "Objects marked Dynamic are not baked, and chapter six follows them"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "plot-mesh"}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 2.2}}
    "Of those meshes, only the surfaces made of triangles are baked, not lines or points"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 2.2 1.2}} "A triangle that misses the box is skipped"}.

One that crosses the box's edge is kept whole, not clipped.

But the bake makes cells for it only inside the box.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.2 2.2}}
    "In the lab, the ceiling panel's light comes from an area light"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 2.2 3.2}}
    "The lab leaves the panel's mesh out of the bake, so the panel has no cells"}.

So the panel's light reaches the cells only through that area light, which chapter four follows.

### The box becomes a cube of cells {#c2-cube kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoBounds panelValues 1.0 {' {showBox 1.0} {boundsStep 0.0} {focusRow 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 28.0 30.0 14.0} {pose 40.0 36.0 16.0}}}}}}}

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "A setting called the subdivision picks how many cells the box gets"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "begin-bake"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 0.0}}
    "At its default, the box's longest axis gets one hundred twenty-eight cells, two to the seventh"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingBeginBake {highlightCode "begin-bake" {' "shrink"}}}}${overText
    {' {boundsStepRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "A shorter axis can get fewer cells"}.

For a shorter axis, the voxelizer starts from the longest side and its one hundred twenty-eight cells, and halves both as long as the halved side still covers that axis.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The box is three point two metres tall, half its six point four metre width"}.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Half of six point four metres is three point two, which still covers the height, so the height's count halves once, to sixty-four"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Half again is one point six metres, which no longer covers the height, so the halving stops"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingBeginBake {highlightCode "begin-bake" {' "cube"}}}}${overText
    {' {boundsStepRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The counts stay, but every side of the octree's box is then stretched to the longest one, so the octree covers a cube"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 2.0 4.0}}
    "So at each level, the octree's cells are cubes of one size"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 4.0 7.0}}
    "The smallest are five centimetres wide, seven levels below the whole cube"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "begin-bake" {' "cell"}}}}The height's sixty-four cells reach only up to three point two metres, the middle of the cube.

${overText {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 3.0}} "So the top half of that cube holds no cells at all"}.

### A triangle goes down the octree {#c2-descent kind=3d density=0.9}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelCode 0.5 {'
    listingPlotFace
    {highlightCode "plot-face" {' "octants"}}
    {workedTriangle 1.0}
    {descentLevel 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {satStep 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.01 0.24 -1.71}}
    {cameraDistance 7.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {path {' cameraDistance cameraYaw} {' {' 5.0 50.0} {' 2.2 58.0} {' 0.9 60.0}} beat}}}}}

${overText {' {descentLevelRange 0.0 0.5}} "Take one triangle on the spout of this teapot"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "plot-face-recurse"}}}${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 0.5 0.75}}
    "The triangle enters the octree at its root, the whole cube"}.

${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 0.75 1.0}}
    "The root is cut into eight children, each one eighth of the cube"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "plot-face" {' "sat"}}}}${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Each child is tested against the triangle"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "plot-face" {' "create"}}}}${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Only the children the triangle overlaps become cells, so the triangle carves a path that goes one level deeper each time"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "plot-face" {' "down"}}}}${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 3.0 7.0}}
    "Seven levels down, the children are five centimetres wide"}.

These smallest cells are called leaves.

${over
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 7.0}}
    {overText
        {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 0.7}}
        "The triangle has created only the handful of cells it touches"}}.

### The test: a separating axis {#c2-sat kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedTriangle 1.0}
    {descentLevel 5.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {satStep 0.0}
    {focusRow 8.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.15 0.15 -1.8}}
    {cameraDistance 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 52.0 22.0} {aim 64.0 28.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "tri-box"}}}${overText
    {' {satStepRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The overlap test projects the child cell and the triangle onto an axis"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 1.2 sentence}}}If the two projections do not overlap on that axis, the shapes cannot touch.

Such an axis is called a separating axis, and the test looks for one.

${overText {' {descentLevelRange 5.0 6.0}} "The test tries three groups of axes"}.

First come nine axes, one for each pairing of a triangle edge with a cell axis.

Each of the nine is at right angles to both the edge and the cell axis of its pair.

${overText {' {satStepRange 1.0 2.0}} "Then come the cell's own three axes"}.

${overText
    {' {satStepRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Last comes the triangle's normal, the direction its plane faces"}.

${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 6.0 5.0}}
    "The first axis that separates them rules the child out, so no cell is made there"}.

One child is drawn in red.

${overText {' {satStepRange 1.0 3.0}} "The readout panel names the axis that rejected it"}.

### Every triangle, sharing the cells {#c2-all kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelValues 1.0 {'
    {workedTriangle 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 3.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}
    {focusRow 3.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.9 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 28.0}
    {cameraDistance 7.4}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 30.0 7.2} {pose 32.0 26.0 6.6} {pose 26.0 28.0 7.0}}}}}}}

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "Every triangle of every mesh goes down the octree the same way"}.

Triangles that reach the same cell share it.

${overText {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 7.0}} "Level by level, the cells shrink and multiply"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 7.0}}
    "The room ends with forty-two thousand two hundred eighty-nine leaves, out of about fifty-six thousand cells in all"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 0.7}} "The cells lie only along the surfaces"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.7 1.4}}
    "So only about four in a hundred of a full grid's million cells become leaves"}.

An octree like this, with most of its space left empty, is called sparse.

### A leaf samples its triangle on four by four columns {#c2-leaf kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelCode 0.2 {'
    listingLeafScan
    {highlightCode "leaf-scan" {' "axis"}}
    {workedTriangle 1.0}
    {workedLeaf 1.0}
    {leafColumns 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.01 0.24 -1.71}}
    {cameraDistance 0.32}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 50.0 22.0} {aim 62.0 30.0} {aim 56.0 26.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "leaf-scan"}}}${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "At a leaf, the voxelizer picks the axis the triangle's plane faces most"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "leaf-scan" {' "columns"}}}}${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 1.0 8.0}}
    "The cell is cut into a four-by-four grid of columns along that axis"}.

${overText {' {leafColumnsRange 8.0 16.0}} "Each column the triangle crosses gives one sample"}.

The leaf keeps three sums, one each for albedo, emission, and normal.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 16.0 12.0}}
    "Each sample adds what the material and the triangle hold at its point to those three sums"}.

${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 12.0 8.0}}
    "Each sample counts for one sixteenth, since the cell has sixteen columns"}.

${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 8.0 12.0}}
    "So a triangle that crosses half the columns adds only half the weight of one that crosses all of them"}.

${overText {' {leafColumnsRange 12.0 14.0}} "The leaf also adds up those shares"}.

That total is the fraction of columns hit, called the leaf's coverage.

The leaf stores that coverage as its alpha.

${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 14.0 16.0}}
    "The next chapter uses that alpha to turn the sums into an average"}.

### Materials become flat colours {#c2-material kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedTriangle 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 0.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.9 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 20.0}
    {cameraPitch 26.0}
    {cameraDistance 6.8}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 14.0 24.0 6.4} {pose 28.0 30.0 6.9}}}}}}}

The colours the leaves receive come from the materials.

${cue "key" {' {callout "material-cache"}}}${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The voxelizer reads each material once, into two tables that every sample then looks up"}.

Each table is an image one hundred twenty-eight texels a side.

${overText {' {cellViewRange 0.0 1.0}} "One table holds the albedo, in linear colour"}.

${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The other holds the emission, scaled by the material's emission energy, its brightness multiplier"}.

The emission is also scaled by the exposure from the VoxelGI's own camera settings, which is one when it has none.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 1.0}} "Here each leaf shows the albedo it received"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.0 2.2}}
    "The room's materials have no textures, only flat colours, so every sample of one mesh is the same"}.

### Next: finishing and packing the cells {#c2-recap kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedTriangle 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 1.0}
    {cutHeight 1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.9 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 20.0}
    {cameraPitch 26.0}
    {cameraDistance 6.8}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 26.0 6.4} {' 16.0 7.0}} beat}}}}}

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "So the bake keeps only the cells that surfaces pass through"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 2.0 3.2}}
    "Each leaf sums what its triangles left in it, shown here by the leaf's coverage"}.

Those sums are not yet what the graphics card reads.

The next chapter finishes the cells and packs them into bytes.

### Where to read {#c2-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="bake-entry, find-meshes, begin-bake, plot-mesh, plot-face-recurse, tri-box, leaf-scan, material-cache"}

These are the voxelizer's functions, in the order the bake calls them.

## What the bake stores {#c3 number=3 target=350 summary="Leaves are averaged, parents emptied, the cells sorted and packed into 48 bytes, and a distance field made; then everything is uploaded." sources="fixup-leaf, fixup-parent, sort-cells, export-octree, export-data, rgbe9995, sdf-edt, sdf-encode, allocate-data, instance-texture"}

### From cells to bytes {#c3-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="BAKE, SDF"}

The bake has left a tree of cells that hold raw sums.

Before the graphics card sees them, the cells are finished, sorted, and packed.

### A leaf becomes an average {#c3-fixup kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelValues 0.2 {'
    {fixupStep 0.0}
    {focusRow -1.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cutHeight 0.3}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.01 0.24 -1.71}}
    {cameraDistance 0.7}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 50.0 20.0 0.6} {pose 60.0 26.0 0.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "fixup-leaf"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "This teapot leaf holds the sums from every triangle that reached it"}.

${overText {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}} "Its alpha is the total of those triangles' shares"}.

${overText {' {fixupStepRange 0.0 1.0}} "Finishing the leaf divides every sum by that alpha"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 6.0}}
    "So the leaf now holds its samples' average albedo and average normal direction"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 6.0 9.0}}
    "After the division, its alpha is set to one, so every leaf counts as fully covered"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingFixup {highlightCode "fixup" {' "fight"}}}}${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "Sometimes the samples' normals point in opposite directions and cancel out"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.3 0.4}}
    "When their average is shorter than one hundredth, the leaf keeps no normal at all"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.4 0.5}}
    "Chapter four's direct lighting then treats that leaf as facing every way, so a light from any side lights it"}.

### Parents keep only their coverage {#c3-parents kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelCode 1.0 {'
    listingFixup
    {highlightCode "fixup" {' "parent"}}
    {octreeLevel 6.0}
    {cellView 1.0}
    {cutHeight 1.2}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.5 -0.6}}
    {cameraYaw 28.0}
    {cameraPitch 30.0}
    {cameraDistance 5.2}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 22.0 28.0 5.0} {pose 36.0 34.0 5.6}}}}}}}

A cell that has children is called a parent.

${cue "key" {' {callout "fixup-parent"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 6.0}}
    "A parent gets no colour at all"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 1.2 1.0}} "Its albedo, emission, and normal are all set to zero"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "fixup" {' "alpha"}}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.0 0.8}}
    "A parent keeps only its alpha, its coverage"}.

That coverage is the sum of its children's alpha, divided by eight.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 0.8 0.5}} "So a parent with four leaves inside it holds one half"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 6.0 4.0}}
    "On a coarser level, a parent spans more open air, so fewer of its eight children exist"}.

So on a surface, a parent's coverage usually drops with each coarser level.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 0.5 1.6}} "The parents get their light later"}.

Every time the lights change, the graphics card rebuilds that light from the leaves.

### Sorted by level, each level in one run {#c3-sort kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelCode 0.0 {'
    listingSortCells
    {highlightCode "sort-cells" {' "key"}}
    {cellArray 1.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.9 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 30.0}
    {cameraDistance 7.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 22.0 30.0} {aim 30.0 32.0}}}}}}}

This chapter showed finishing first, but the bake actually sorts the cells before it finishes them.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sort-cells"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "It sorts them by level first, and then by position"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "sort-cells" {' "root"}}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "So the root comes first, at index zero"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "sort-cells" {' "remap"}}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "Sorting moves the cells, so each parent's list of child indices is rewritten to point at the children's new places"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "sort-cells" {' "runs"}}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 7.0}}
    "Each level now fills one unbroken run of the sorted array of cells, shown here in its own colour"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "level-counts"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 5.0}}
    "The bake also hands over how many cells each level has"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 4.0}}
    "When Godot draws a frame, its renderer uses those counts to find where each level's run starts and ends, so it can work on one level at a time"}.

### Forty-eight bytes a cell, in two records {#c3-bytes kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelValues 0.2 {'
    {cellArray 0.0}
    {fixupStep 1.0}
    {focusRow 1.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 0.0}
    {cutHeight 0.3}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.01 0.24 -1.71}}
    {cameraDistance 0.7}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 54.0 20.0 0.6} {pose 62.0 26.0 0.8} {pose 56.0 22.0 0.7}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "export-octree"}}}Each cell is stored as two records.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The first record holds its eight children's indices, in thirty-two bytes"}.

An index with every bit set to one means that child is empty.

${cue "key" {' {callout "export-data"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The second record is sixteen bytes"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "It starts with the cell's position, measured in cells of the cell's own size"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "The three coordinates are packed into eleven, ten, and eleven bits, so the position fits in four bytes"}.

${overText {' {focusRowRange 3.0 4.0}} "Next, three bytes store the albedo"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 4.0 5.0}}
    "A fourth byte stores the coverage, the alpha from chapter two"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "rgbe9995"}}}${over
    {' {focusRowRange 5.0 6.0}}
    {overText
        {' {cellViewRange 0.0 3.0}}
        "The emission takes the next four bytes, with one nine-bit number for each colour channel"}}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 6.0 7.0}}
    "The last five bits hold an exponent, which scales all three numbers by one power of two"}.

So emission brighter than white still fits in four bytes.

${overText {' {cellViewRange 3.0 2.0}} "This room has no emission, so those bytes are zero here"}.

${overText {' {focusRowRange 7.0 8.0}} "Last comes its normal, as three signed bytes, one per axis"}.

${overText {' {focusRowRange 8.0 9.0}} "The normal's fourth byte is left unused"}.

So the two records take forty-eight bytes a cell.

### The distance field, exact, in three passes {#c3-field kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoField panelValues 0.0 {' {slice 1.0} {edtPass 0.0} {sliceHeight 0.3}}}}

The bake makes one more thing, the distance field from chapter one.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdf-edt"}}}${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.6 0.45}}
    "It covers the box with a grid of places, each the size of a leaf"}.

For each place, it measures how far the nearest leaf is.

${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.45 0.3}}
    "That distance field will let shadow rays skip through empty space"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingDistanceField {highlightCode "distance-field" {' "seed"}}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Leaves start at a distance of zero"}.

Every other place starts far away, shown white.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "distance-field" {' "z"}}}}${overText
    {' {edtPassRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Then a first pass walks along each column of places that runs in z"}.

It finds each place's distance to the nearest leaf in that column.

${over
    {' {edtPassRange 1.0 3.0}}
    {cat {'
        {cue "key" {' {highlightCode "distance-field" {' "y"}}}}
        {text "It runs again along y, "}
        {cue "key" {' {highlightCode "distance-field" {' "x"}}}}
        {text "and then along x"}}}}.

${overText
    {' {edtPassRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "In each later pass, a place looks at every other place on its line along that pass's axis"}.

For each of them, it adds the squared distance between the two to what the earlier passes found there.

The place keeps the smallest of these sums.

${overText
    {' {edtPassRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "So after the third pass, each place holds the exact squared distance to its nearest leaf"}.

Together, these three passes are called an exact distance transform.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdf-encode"} panelValues}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Each place keeps its distance in a single byte"}.

${overText {' {focusRowRange 3.0 4.0}} "A leaf stores zero"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "Any other place takes its distance in whole cells, rounded down, and adds one"}.

So only a leaf ever stores zero.

${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.3 1.0}}
    "Here, at the height of the sphere's centre, the field forms a ring around each object"}.

${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 1.0 0.6}}
    "In the next chapter, shadow rays use those distances to skip through empty space"}.

### What the GPU keeps {#c3-upload kind=3d density=0.4}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelValues 0.5 {'
    {slice 2.0}
    {mipLevel 0.0}
    {sliceHeight 0.0}
    {cellArray 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.4 0.0}}
    {cameraYaw 10.0}
    {cameraPitch 60.0}
    {cameraDistance 8.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 0.0 70.0} {aim 16.0 58.0}}}}}}}

Now the bake's data goes to the graphics card.

${cue "key" {' {callout "allocate-data"}}}${overText
    {' {sliceRange 2.0 1.0}}
    "The upload turns the two records into two storage buffers"}.

${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "It turns the distance field into a three-dimensional texture, with one byte per texel"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdf-gpu-disabled"}}}Godot's source also holds a version that builds the field on the graphics card, but that version is compiled out.

So the shadow rays always read the field the bake made on the processor.

${cue "key" {' {callout "instance-texture"}}}${overText
    {' {sliceRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Each VoxelGI in the renderer then gets a colour texture for its light, which the light passes fill"}.

${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 1.0 0.1}}
    "At its finest, this light texture has one texel for every leaf-sized place in the box"}.

${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "The light texture has one mip for each octree level, each mip half the size of the one before"}.

Beside the light texture, a buffer keeps each cell's light at full precision, in four floats.

Chapter six rewrites the texture from that buffer, without lighting the cells again.

${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 3.0 0.0}}
    "In the lab, the light texture's finest mip takes four megabytes"}.

All the cells take under three megabytes, less than that one mip.

The distance field takes one megabyte.

### Next: light {#c3-recap kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {slice 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 2.0}
    {cutHeight 1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.5 -0.6}}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 30.0}
    {cameraDistance 5.4}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 32.0 5.0} {' 20.0 5.6}} beat}}}}}

${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 2.0 0.0}}
    "So every leaf now describes its surface, with its albedo and normal"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The distance field stores how far each place is from the nearest leaf"}.

None of it has been lit yet.

The next chapter lights the leaves.

### Where to read {#c3-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="fixup-leaf, fixup-parent, sort-cells, export-octree, export-data, rgbe9995, sdf-edt, sdf-encode, allocate-data, instance-texture"}

These are the lines that finish, pack, measure, and upload the cells.

## Lighting the cells {#c4 number=4 target=300 summary="When the lights change, each leaf gets its direct light, shadowed through the field, and the texture stores it over the dynamic range." sources="relight-gate, light-cache, max-lights, light-upload, compute-light, light-at-pos, raymarch, area-light, write-texture"}

### Light passes, on change {#c4-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="LIGHT, TEX"}

The cells now hold what each surface is.

The light passes from chapter one now give each cell the light it sends back out.

### Only when the data or a light changes {#c4-when kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelValues 1.0 {'
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 1.0}
    {lightX 0.9}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 5.0}
    {cutHeight 0.2}
    {focusRow -1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 0.0 18.0 4.6} {pose 12.0 24.0 4.4}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "relight-gate"}}}The light passes do not run every frame.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange -1.0 0.0}}
    "They run only when a light changes, when the bake is redone, or when one of three VoxelGI settings changes"}.

Those three settings are the dynamic range, the propagation, and whether the second bounce is on.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-cache"}}}${overText
    {' {lightXRange 0.9 0.4}}
    "Every frame, the culling from chapter one also checks each visible light"}.

${overText
    {' {lightXRange 0.4 0.2}}
    "For each VoxelGI in view, it compares that light's settings with a copy saved earlier"}.

${overText
    {' {lightXRange 0.2 -0.6}}
    "Move a light, and the cells are lit again, here the floor under its new position"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "max-lights"}}}At most thirty-two lights take part, which is the engine's limit.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-upload"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Each light's position and reach are first converted into the box's cells, the unit in which the cells' positions and the distance field are stored"}.

### A cell's direct light, step by step {#c4-direct kind=3d density=0.9}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedLight 1.0}
    {lightStep 0.0}
    {marchSteps 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 2.0 16.0} {aim 10.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "compute-light"}}}${overText
    {' {lightHeightRange 1.8 1.6}}
    "Take this floor cell beside the sphere, the ringed one"}.

${overText
    {' {lightHeightRange 1.6 1.4}}
    "The lab adds an extra omni light here, a point light that shines in every direction"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingComputeLight {highlightCode "compute-light" {' "vector"}}}}${overText
    {' {lightStepRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The first of the light passes is called the direct light pass, and it finds the direction between the cell and the light"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "omni-atten"} panelValues}}${over
    {' {lightStepRange 1.0 2.0}}
    {overText
        {' {lightXRange 0.9 0.5}}
        "It also works out how much the light falls off over the distance between them"}}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-at-pos"}}}${overText
    {' {lightStepRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "If the cell's normal faces away from the light, the cell gets nothing from it"}.

${overText
    {' {lightXRange 0.5 0.9}}
    "Otherwise, the direct light pass weights the light by the cosine between the cell's normal and the direction to the light"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingComputeLight {highlightCode "compute-light" {' "result"}}}}${overText
    {' {lightStepRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "That weighted light times the cell's albedo, plus the cell's emission, gives the light the cell sends back out"}.

### The shadow ray steps through the distance field {#c4-shadow kind=3d density=0.9}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelCode 0.3 {'
    listingRaymarch
    {highlightCode "raymarch" {' "loop"}}
    {workedLight 1.0}
    {lightStep 4.0}
    {marchSteps 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 0.0 16.0 4.4} {pose 8.0 20.0 4.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "raymarch"}}}${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "When the light casts shadows, a ray starts on the light's side and marches back toward the cell through the distance field"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "raymarch" {' "read"}}}}${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "At each step, the ray reads from the field how far the nearest surface is, shown as the circle"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "raymarch" {' "step"}}}}${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Then it jumps that far"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "The jump is rounded down to whole cells, but it is never shorter than one cell"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 4.0 6.0}}
    "Those cells are counted along the axis the ray moves along most"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "raymarch" {' "solid"}}}}${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 6.0 20.0}}
    "Here the ray reaches the sphere's cells and reads a leaf, where the field is zero"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 20.0 40.0}}
    "So the floor cell is in shadow, and it gets none of this light"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "raymarch" {' "partial"}}}}${overText
    {' {lightZRange -0.4 -0.3}}
    "A ray that passes close to a surface without reaching it can read less than one cell"}.

${overText
    {' {lightZRange -0.3 -0.25}}
    "The field gives such readings because it is blended between neighbouring texels"}.

${overText
    {' {lightZRange -0.25 -0.2}}
    "The floor cell's light is then multiplied by the smallest reading the ray met, so it is dimmed instead of blocked"}.

${overText {' {lightZRange -0.2 -0.1}} "That gives the shadow a slightly soft edge"}.

### The panel's area light has no shadow {#c4-area kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelValues 0.5 {'
    {workedLight 1.0}
    {lightStep 4.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.0 1.2 -0.5}}
    {cameraDistance 5.6}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 0.0 16.0} {aim 10.0 22.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "area-light"}}}${overText
    {' {lightStepRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The ceiling panel is an area light, a glowing rectangle rather than a point"}.

${overText
    {' {lightStepRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Its light on a cell is an integral over the whole rectangle"}.

${overText {' {lightStepRange 3.0 4.0}} "VoxelGI does not sum the rectangle point by point"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Instead, it computes the integral in closed form, as a sum over the rectangle's edges"}.

This method is called linearly transformed cosines.

For diffuse light, it integrates the plain cosine over the rectangle.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.2 0.7}}
    "The area light never marches a ray through the distance field"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.7 1.2}}
    "So the panel lights the cells under the sphere as if the sphere were not there"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "ltc"}}}Godot's own direct lighting, which shades surfaces straight from the lights, divides this integral by two pi.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "The code shows that VoxelGI leaves that division out, so its integral is two pi times larger than the one direct lighting uses"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "So the cells take the panel's light at a different scale from the surfaces that direct lighting shades"}.

### Every leaf, lit {#c4-all kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 5.0}
    {cutHeight 0.2}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.6 -0.4}}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 34.0}
    {cameraDistance 7.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 12.0 32.0 6.6} {pose 28.0 38.0 7.2}}}}}}}

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.2 1.6}}
    "The direct light pass runs these steps for every leaf, sixty-four leaves to a workgroup by the engine's choice"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 1.6 3.2}} "The room's cells pick up the panel's light"}.

Cells the panel sees are bright.

Cells it cannot see stay dark, waiting for the bounce in the next chapter.

### The texture stores light over the dynamic range {#c4-write kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelValues 0.5 {'
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {dynamicRange 2.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 4.0}
    {cutHeight 0.2}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.1 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 14.0}
    {cameraPitch 48.0}
    {cameraDistance 3.4}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 8.0 50.0} {aim 20.0 44.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "write-texture"} {focusRow 2.0}}}${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 5.0 4.0}}
    "The last pass, the write pass, stores each cell's light in that cell's texel of the three-dimensional texture"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.0 sentence}}}Each channel of a texel has only eight bits, for values from zero to one.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "So the pass first divides the light by a scale called the dynamic range"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "The dynamic range is two by default, so light up to twice white survives"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicRangeRange 2.0 1.0}}
    "At a range of one, the bright cells under the panel clip to white, and the readout panel's count of clipped leaves goes up"}.

${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 4.0 1.0} {octreeLevelRange 7.0 6.0}}
    "The alpha channel stores the cell's coverage, one for a leaf and less for a sparse parent"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.2 0.6}}
    "Texels with no cell stay at zero, fully transparent, so a cone sees straight through them"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "unused-push"}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.6 0.8}}
    "Godot's VoxelGI stores no direction with its light, so each texel holds one light for all directions"}.

The code shows that the light passes still receive an anisotropy value, a strength for light that changes with direction.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw -40.0 sentence}}}But no line of the shader reads it, so the stored light stays the same in every direction.

### Next: the bounce {#c4-recap kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 5.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.6 -0.4}}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 34.0}
    {cameraDistance 7.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 26.0 6.6} {' 14.0 7.2}} beat}}}}}

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 1.8}} "So every leaf now holds the direct light it sends out"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.8 0.4}}
    "Every shadow in the cells' direct light comes from marching the distance field"}.

The area light casts none.

So far, each cell holds only direct light, which comes straight from the lights.

The next chapter lets the cells light each other.

### Where to read {#c4-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="relight-gate, light-cache, max-lights, light-upload, compute-light, light-at-pos, raymarch, area-light, write-texture"}

These are the light passes' lines, from the check that decides when they run to the texture.

## The second bounce and the mips {#c5 number=5 target=205 summary="The coarse levels are filled from their children, then six cones from every leaf gather the light around it." sources="update-mipmaps, pass-order, second-bounce, bounce-cones, bounce-march, bounce-restore"}

### The texture reads itself back {#c5-map kind=2d density=0.4 graph=frame focus="LIGHT, TEX"}

So far, only the cells the lights reach send light back out.

That light has bounced off one surface, so it counts as the first bounce.

Another of the light passes, the bounce pass, reads back the texture the write pass filled, so the cells light each other.

### A parent's light from its children {#c5-mips kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoMips panelCode 0.3 {'
    listingUpdateMipmaps
    {highlightCode "update-mipmaps" {' "children"}}
    {slice 2.0}
    {mipLevel 0.0}
    {workedParent 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 6.0} {' -6.0} {' 4.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "update-mipmaps"}}}${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "A cone widens as it travels, so it must read the texture's coarser mips, one for each octree level"}.

${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 3.0 0.0}}
    "The direct light pass lit only the leaves, so a pass called the mip pass fills in the parents' light first"}.

${overText {' {mipLevelRange 0.0 1.0}} "Each parent sums the light of its children"}.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Then it divides that sum by a divisor"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "The divisor lies between eight and the number of children the parent really has"}.

${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "A setting called propagation decides where the divisor falls, as the next beat shows"}.

${overText {' {mipLevelRange 3.0 4.0}} "Level by level, each mip holds a coarser copy of the light"}.

${overText {' {mipLevelRange 4.0 7.0}} "The coarsest mip is a single texel for the whole box"}.

### Propagation sets the divisor {#c5-prop kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoMips panelValues 0.3 {'
    {slice 2.0}
    {mipLevel 2.0}
    {workedParent 1.0}
    {propagation 0.5}
    {focusRow 3.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' -4.0} {' 6.0}} beat}}}}}

${overText {' {propagationRange 0.5 0.25}} "At a propagation of zero, the divisor is always eight"}.

${overText
    {' {propagationRange 0.25 0.0}}
    "So empty children count as black, and the coarse levels darken"}.

${overText
    {' {propagationRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "At a propagation of one, only the children that exist are averaged, so the coarse levels keep their brightness"}.

${overText
    {' {propagationRange 1.0 0.5}}
    "At the default of one half, a parent with four children divides by six, halfway between eight and four"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "So a higher propagation keeps the coarse levels brighter, and the wide cones that read them pick up more light"}.

### Six cones from every leaf {#c5-cones kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoBounce panelCode 0.3 {'
    listingSecondBounce
    {highlightCode "second-bounce" {' "frame"}}
    {workedBounce 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {coneSteps 6.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 34.0 28.0} {aim 48.0 32.0} {aim 40.0 30.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "second-bounce"}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 2.0 6.0}}
    "Take this floor cell near the red wall, the ringed one"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "second-bounce" {' "cones"}}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 6.0 4.0}}
    "The bounce pass builds three axes around the cell's normal"}.

It aims every cone relative to those three axes.

${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "It sends one cone straight along the normal, with a weight of one quarter"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "bounce-cones"}}}${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 1.0 6.0}}
    "Five more cones lean sixty degrees away from the normal, each with a weight of point one five, so the six weights add up to one"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 6.0 3.0}}
    "Each cone's edge lies thirty degrees from the cone's centre line"}.

### Each cone widens and reads coarser mips {#c5-march kind=3d density=0.9}

${cue "key" {opening demoBounce panelCode 0.3 {'
    listingSecondBounce
    {highlightCode "second-bounce" {' "march"}}
    {workedBounce 1.0}
    {conesShown 1.0}
    {coneSteps 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 40.0 2.4} {' 30.0 3.2}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "bounce-march"}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "A cone starts one and a half cells from the cell's centre, clear of the cell itself"}.

${cue "key" {' runMarchCone {runSeconds 2.5} {runFrom 1.0} {runTo 3.0}}}The cone widens as it goes, and on each step it advances by half its current width.

${cue "key" {'
    {highlightCode "second-bounce" {' "lod"}}
    runMarchCone
    {runSeconds 2.5}
    {runFrom 3.0}
    {runTo 6.0}}}At each step, it reads the mip whose texels match that width.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "The cone blends its samples front to back, so each new sample lies behind those before it"}.

${cue "key" {' {focusRow 2.0}}}A sample's alpha, its coverage, makes the cone more opaque, so samples farther along add less, like layers of glass.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "The cone stops once it is ninety-five percent opaque, or when it runs out of distance"}.

${cue "key" {' {stop {' "marchCone"}}}}${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 1.0 6.0}}
    "The last of the six cones never gets that opaque, and ends after eleven steps only about two thirds opaque"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 6.0 11.0}}
    "The room's cells are thin shells around open air, so this cone runs out of distance first"}.

### The bounce is added, and everything rebuilt {#c5-restore kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelCode 1.0 {'
    listingSecondBounce
    {highlightCode "second-bounce" {' "restore"}}
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 4.0}
    {cutHeight 0.2}
    {bounces 1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.4 0.4 0.2}}
    {cameraYaw 40.0}
    {cameraPitch 34.0}
    {cameraDistance 4.2}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 34.0 32.0 4.0} {pose 48.0 36.0 4.6}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "bounce-restore"}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.2 0.5}}
    "Each cone's light is multiplied by its weight, and by the albedo of the cell that sent the cones, because the light bounces off that cell"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.5 0.65}}
    "The cone's light is also multiplied by the dynamic range, which undoes the write pass's division"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.65 0.8}}
    "The six results are summed and added to the cell's direct light"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pass-order"}}}Then the mip pass and the write pass run again.

${overText {' {bouncesRange 1.0 2.0}} "The floor cells beside the red wall brighten"}.

Only the leaves trace bounce cones.

They trace them only once, so light bounces off at most two surfaces, and there is no third bounce.

### Next: what moves {#c5-recap kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 4.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}
    {bounces 2.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.6 -0.4}}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 34.0}
    {cameraDistance 7.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 28.0 6.6} {' 16.0 7.2}} beat}}}}}

${over
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 5.0}}
    {overText
        {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 1.0}}
        "So the texture now holds both bounces of light, at every level from five-centimetre cells to the whole box"}}.

All of it comes from objects that stand still.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 1.0 3.2}} "The next chapter adds one that moves"}.

### Where to read {#c5-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="update-mipmaps, pass-order, second-bounce, bounce-cones, bounce-march, bounce-restore"}

These are the mip and bounce passes, and the order they run in.

## Moving objects {#c6 number=6 target=260 summary="An object marked Dynamic is drawn from six sides every frame, lit, shrunk, and plotted into the same texture." sources="gi-mode-flags, dynamic-list, dynamic-oversample, dynamic-views, material-outputs, dynamic-lighting, shrink, plot-blend, dynamic-clear"}

### Dynamic objects write into the texture {#c6-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="DYN, TEX"}

The bake knows only what stood still.

The bake runs on the processor over every mesh in the room, and it does not run each frame.

So an object marked Dynamic is drawn into the texture every frame instead.

### Dynamic objects are not baked {#c6-mode kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelValues 0.5 {'
    {dynamicVoxels 0.0}
    {dynamicView -1.0}
    {dynamicX 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 50.0 1.8} {' 60.0 2.2}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "gi-mode-flags"}}}Here the teapot's GI mode is Dynamic, so the bake leaves it out.

${overText
    {' {dynamicMipRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The lab's room was baked without it, so the teapot has no cells and no baked light"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-list"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicVoxelsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Each dynamic object is drawn into the texture in every frame where the room's VoxelGI is in view"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.0 0.3}}
    "That happens whether the object moved or not, so it costs time even when the object stands still"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.3 0.6}}
    "The points on screen are the texels the teapot wrote into the texture"}.

This write is called plotting, and it blends each texel over the light already stored there.

${overText
    {' {dynamicVoxelsRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The lab draws each point four times brighter than its texel's light, so the points look brighter than the light the teapot really adds"}.

${over
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.6 0.0}}
    {overText
        {' {dynamicVoxelsRange 0.0 1.0}}
        "These points come from a copy of the plot that the lab runs on the processor"}}.

The plot that fills the texture runs on the graphics card each frame, so the texture the room is shaded from holds the teapot too.

### Six views, sixteen texels a cell {#c6-views kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelValues 0.5 {'
    {dynamicVoxels 0.0}
    {dynamicView 0.0}
    {shrinkLevel 0.0}
    {dynamicX 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 48.0 18.0} {aim 62.0 24.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-oversample"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.3 0.15}}
    "The object is drawn into flat images that cover its bounding box"}.

In these images, each cell is sixteen texels across.

${overText {' {dynamicXRange 0.15 0.0}} "Later these texels are averaged down to one per cell"}.

${overText
    {' {shrinkLevelRange 0.0 4.0}}
    "The average alpha then says how much of each cell the object covers"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-views"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicViewRange 0.0 5.0}}
    "The object is drawn six times, with orthographic views along each axis in both directions, so no side is missed"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "material-outputs"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicViewRange 5.0 0.0}}
    "Each view records the material's albedo, normal, emission, and depth at every texel"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-lighting"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicViewRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "A texel's depth gives its position along the view, and so its cell in the box"}.

${overText
    {' {shrinkLevelRange 4.0 0.0}}
    "Each texel is then lit like a cell, with shadows from the baked distance field alone"}.

### Shrunk two by two, plotted at each mip {#c6-shrink kind=3d density=0.9}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelCode 0.5 {'
    listingDynamicObject
    {highlightCode "dynamic-object" {' "shrink"}}
    {dynamicVoxels 0.0}
    {dynamicView 0.0}
    {shrinkLevel 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 54.0} {' 64.0} {' 56.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "shrink"}}}${overText
    {' {shrinkLevelRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Next, the shrink pass halves the lit view again and again, and averages each two-by-two block of texels into one"}.

${overText
    {' {shrinkLevelRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "It goes from sixteen texels a cell to eight, then four, then two, then one"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "dynamic-object" {' "plot"}}}}${overText
    {' {shrinkLevelRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "At one texel a cell, the shrink pass plots each texel into the texture's finest mip"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "plot-blend"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicVoxelsRange 0.0 0.5}}
    "Along the direction the view looks, a texel's depth usually lands between two cells"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicVoxelsRange 0.5 1.0}}
    "So the texel is split between those two cells, and the nearer cell gets the larger share"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.0 0.05}}
    "Several texels can land in one cell, and each share is blended over what is already there"}.

${overText {' {shrinkLevelRange 4.0 11.0}} "The halving goes on past one texel a cell"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicMipRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "Each further halving plots its texels into the next, coarser mip"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicMipRange 2.0 4.0}}
    "Unlike the four halvings down to one texel a cell, these divide the light by the divisor that propagation sets, as the mip pass does"}.

### The plotted voxels, mip by mip {#c6-mips kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelValues 0.5 {'
    {dynamicVoxels 1.0}
    {dynamicView -1.0}
    {dynamicMip 0.0}
    {dynamicX 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 46.0 18.0 1.8} {pose 58.0 24.0 2.4}}}}}}}

At the finest mip, the teapot is plotted in five-centimetre cells.

${overText {' {dynamicMipRange 0.0 1.0}} "One mip up, the cells are ten centimetres wide"}.

${overText {' {dynamicMipRange 1.0 3.0}} "Each mip halves the detail again"}.

These coarser mips are what the wider cones will read.

### Rewritten every frame, never bounced {#c6-cost kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelValues 0.5 {'
    {dynamicVoxels 1.0}
    {dynamicView -1.0}
    {dynamicMip 0.0}
    {dynamicX 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 50.0 20.0 1.6} {pose 64.0 26.0 2.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-clear"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.0 0.15}}
    "Each frame, the whole texture is cleared before the teapot is plotted"}.

${overText {' {dynamicXRange 0.15 0.3}} "Then only the write pass runs"}.

It writes each cell's light back from the float buffer of chapter three, where the light passes left it at full precision.

${overText
    {' {dynamicMipRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "So the dynamic texels exist only for this frame, at every mip"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.3 0.4}}
    "The bounce already ran in the light passes, before the teapot was plotted"}.

${overText {' {dynamicXRange 0.4 0.45}} "So no light bounces off the teapot"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.45 0.5}}
    "It casts no shadow in VoxelGI's light either, because the baked distance field does not contain it"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicMipRange 3.0 0.0}}
    "Every frame, each object costs six draws, one per view, and seventy-two compute dispatches"}.

${cue "key" {' {focusRow 1.0}}}That makes twelve dispatches for each of the six views.

${overText
    {' {dynamicMipRange 0.0 7.0}}
    "One lights the view, four halve it down to one texel a cell, and seven more halve it through the lab's seven coarser mips"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-light-count"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.5 0.0}}
    "Chapter four's limit of thirty-two lights does not hold here"}.

The code shows that the pass that lights the views has no such limit.

So past thirty-two lights, it reads beyond the end of the array that holds them, and what it reads there is undefined.

### Next: reading the texture {#c6-recap kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {dynamicVoxels 1.0}
    {dynamicView -1.0}
    {dynamicX 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 48.0 1.8} {' 62.0 2.4}} beat}}}}}

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.0 0.4}}
    "So a moving object shows up in the same texture, redrawn every frame"}.

The object is lit directly, but no light bounces off it.

Static or moving, everything ends in that one texture.

The next chapter reads it back, one pixel at a time.

### Where to read {#c6-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="gi-mode-flags, dynamic-list, dynamic-oversample, dynamic-views, dynamic-lighting, shrink, plot-blend, dynamic-clear"}

These are the lines of the dynamic objects' path.

## Reading it per pixel {#c7 number=7 target=310 summary="The pre-pass tags each pixel with its VoxelGIs; the screen pass moves it into the box and traces four cones and a reflection cone." sources="pairing-two, instance-ubo, tag-write, process-gi, voxel-gi-compute, edge-blend, cones-low, cone-trace-45, cones-high, cone-trace, spec-cone, range-restore"}

### The screen pass reads the texture {#c7-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="PRE, CONE, TEX"}

The texture is now full of light.

The question is how a pixel reads that light cheaply.

The screen pass answers it, every frame.

### Each pixel knows its VoxelGIs {#c7-tag kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.5 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {specCone 0.0}
    {focusRow -1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 36.0 14.0} {aim 46.0 18.0}}}}}}}

First, the screen pass needs to know which VoxelGIs light each pixel.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pairing-two"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 10.0 30.0}}
    "Each object keeps the first two VoxelGIs it overlaps, so a third one never lights it"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "tag-write"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange -1.0 0.0}}
    "A pass that draws depth before the main shading carries those two VoxelGIs to the object's pixels"}.

That pass is called the depth pre-pass.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 75.0}}
    "It writes those two VoxelGIs' indices into every pixel it draws"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 75.0 90.0}}
    "Each index takes one byte, so the pair takes two bytes per pixel"}.

An object with no VoxelGI writes two hundred fifty-five, the largest value a byte holds, to mean none.

${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 30.0 20.0}}
    "So the screen pass knows, for each pixel, which VoxelGIs to read"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "instance-ubo"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 90.0 60.0}}
    "Besides the indices, the screen pass needs each VoxelGI's settings, which it reads from one buffer"}.

That buffer holds up to eight VoxelGIs in view, and each pixel names at most two of them.

${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 20.0 10.0}}
    "In Godot, this sphere's pixels would all name the room's one VoxelGI"}.

### Into the box's cells {#c7-space kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {pixelAzimuth 60.0}
    {focusRow 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 40.0 3.4} {' 32.0 3.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "voxel-gi-compute"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 30.0 10.0}}
    "Take this ringed pixel, on the side of the sphere that faces the red wall"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 100.0}}
    "The screen pass converts the pixel's position and normal into the box's own coordinates, counted in cells"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 100.0 85.0}}
    "A pixel outside the box gets nothing from this VoxelGI"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "edge-blend"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The pass then multiplies the light this VoxelGI gives the pixel by a weight called the edge fade"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 85.0 60.0}}
    "The fade is one at the box's centre and falls to zero at its faces"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 10.0 20.0}}
    "Afterwards, the pass adds up the fades of all the pixel's boxes, and divides the weighted light by that sum"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 20.0 30.0}}
    "So where two boxes overlap, each box's share of the light follows its fade"}.

But with one box, that sum is just that box's fade.

So multiplying by the fade and dividing by it cancel out.

${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 30.0 40.0}}
    "Only a pixel exactly on a face, where the fade is zero, loses the box's light"}.

### Four cones at forty-five degrees {#c7-low kind=3d density=0.9}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {coneSteps 16.0}
    {focusRow 3.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 40.0 16.0} {aim 30.0 22.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "cones-low"}}}${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 0.0 4.0}}
    "At the default quality, four cones leave the pixel, each tilted forty-five degrees from its normal"}.

Each cone has a weight of one quarter.

${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 4.0 1.0}}
    "Each cone is also wide, forty-five degrees from its axis to its edge"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cone-trace-45"}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "At that angle, the cone's radius always equals the distance it has travelled"}.

${overText {' {coneStepsRange 1.0 2.0}} "Each step moves the cone forward by that radius"}.

${cue "key" {' {focusRow 4.0}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Since the radius equals the distance travelled, each step doubles that distance"}.

${cue "key" {' {focusRow 6.0}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "The cone then reads one mip coarser, where a texel is twice as wide"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 4.0 6.0}}
    "That texel already combines the light of everything in the cone's footprint, so one sample stands in for many"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 72.0}}
    "The samples are blended front to back, as in the bounce"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 72.0 85.0}}
    "The cone stops when its alpha, its coverage, reaches one, or when it leaves the box or runs out of distance"}.

### Six cones at High {#c7-high kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 6.0}
    {coneSteps 16.0}
    {quality 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 34.0 18.0} {aim 44.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "cones-high"}}}${overText
    {' {qualityRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "At High quality, the pixel traces the bounce's six cones instead"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 16.0 10.0}}
    "Each of those cones is narrower, thirty degrees from its axis to its edge instead of forty-five"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cone-trace"}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 10.0 4.0}}
    "Each still steps by its radius, and a narrower cone has a smaller radius, so its steps are smaller"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 4.0 16.0}}
    "So the six cones take more samples, each from a finer mip"}.

${overText {' {qualityRange 1.0 0.0}} "At the default, four wide cones do the work"}.

### One more cone for the reflection {#c7-spec kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {specCone 1.0}
    {quality 0.0}
    {sphereRoughness 0.5}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 38.0 16.0} {aim 30.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "spec-cone"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 80.0}}
    "The reflection gets one more cone, along the mirror direction"}.

${overText {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.5 0.3}} "That cone's width follows the surface's roughness"}.

The screen pass squares the roughness first, so a middling roughness of one half narrows the cone as if it were a quarter.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.3 0.15}}
    "On a smooth sphere, the cone is narrow and travels far"}.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.15 0.9}}
    "On a rough one, it opens out to about seventy degrees from its axis"}.

${over
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.9 0.5}}
    {overText
        {' {pixelAzimuthRange 80.0 60.0}}
        "The sphere's reflection of the room follows the roughness, from sharp to blurred"}}.

### The range restored, the coverage kept {#c7-scale kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 4.0}
    {specCone 1.0}
    {interior 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 36.0} {' 44.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "range-restore"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 68.0}}
    "The cones' light is multiplied back by the dynamic range, the scale the texture divided it by"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 68.0 75.0}}
    "The light is also multiplied by the VoxelGI's energy, a brightness setting"}.

${cue "key" {'
    panelCode
    listingVoxelGiCompute
    {highlightCode "voxel-gi-compute" {' "interior"}}
    {viewMode 3.0}}}The cones' alpha, their coverage, says how much of their view they found filled.

${cue "key" {' {viewMode 0.0}}}Later, where that alpha is low, the opaque pass mixes in the environment's light, here the sky's.

The baked data also has a setting called interior, which is off by default.

${overText {' {interiorRange 0.0 1.0}} "With the interior setting on, that alpha is forced to one"}.

So with interior on, the sky's light never mixes in.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}${overText
    {' {interiorRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "That alpha is stored with the light in the screen pass's two buffers, one for ambient light and one for reflection"}.

### A check that is always true {#c7-quirk kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelCode 0.3 {'
    listingVoxelGiCompute
    {highlightCode "voxel-gi-compute" {' "space"}}
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 4.0}
    {specCone 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 40.0} {' 32.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "normal-length-quirk"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 10.0 15.0}}
    "Before it traces any cone, the screen pass checks that the pixel's normal is valid"}.

It tests whether the normal's length is above one half.

${overText {' {pixelElevationRange 15.0 20.0}} "But that test is always true"}.

In GLSL, the language of Godot's renderer shaders, a vector's length method returns its component count, three.

So the check never rejects a pixel, whatever its normal.

### Next: into the image {#c7-recap kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 4.0}
    {specCone 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 30.0 3.4} {' 44.0 3.8}} beat}}}}}

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 90.0}}
    "So every pixel of this room traces four cones and a reflection cone through the texture"}.

Each pixel also keeps the cones' alpha, how much of their view they found filled.

The light and its alpha go into buffers, which the opaque pass turns into colour.

### Where to read {#c7-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="pairing-two, tag-write, process-gi, voxel-gi-compute, edge-blend, cones-low, cone-trace-45, cones-high, cone-trace, spec-cone, range-restore"}

These are the screen pass's lines, from the indices written per pixel to the cones' light multiplied back by the dynamic range.

## Keeping it fast {#c8 number=8 target=440 summary="The bill, and how Godot keeps it small: light passes only on change, over occupied cells only, a level at a time; a few cone samples per pixel; half resolution." sources="relight-gate, update-gate, light-cache, sort-cells, level-dispatch, local-size, pass-order, raymarch, cones-low, cones-high, quality-doc, spec-cone, half-res-setting, half-res-buffers, half-res-shift, half-res-dispatch, gi-buffer-fetch, half-res-doc"}

### Three rates {#c8-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="BAKE, LIGHT, CONE"}

Before the opaque pass turns those buffers into colour, this chapter turns to cost.

It asks what all of this costs, and how Godot keeps that cost small.

The work comes at three rates.

### Once {#c8-map-bake kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="BAKE"}

The bake runs once, usually in the editor.

### On change {#c8-map-light kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="LIGHT"}

The light passes run only when something changes.

### Every frame {#c8-map-cone kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="CONE"}

The screen pass runs every frame.

### Reading the panel {#c8-panel kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelPerformance 1.0 {'
    unhighlight
    {giRelightEachFrame 0.0}
    {quality 0.0}
    {giHalfResolution 0.0}
    {costSplit 0.0}
    {viewMode 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 12.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 24.0 20.0 6.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {highlightPasses {' "scene pass"}}}}The performance panel on the right times the graphics card's work.

${overText
    {' {giPropagationRange 0.5 0.52}}
    "Its line is each frame's time on the graphics card, so a spike is a frame that did more work"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightPasses {' "voxelgiGiCS"}}}}${overText
    {' {giPropagationRange 0.52 0.54}}
    "Below the line, each row is one pass, averaged over the last frames"}.

A row shows the pass's name, a bar for its share of the frame, and its average time in milliseconds.

${cue "key" {' {highlightPasses {' "scene pass" "voxelgiGiCS"}}}}The row called scene pass is the lab's pass that shades the room itself.

It takes most of the frame.

${over
    {' {qualityRange 0.0 1.0}}
    {cat {'
        {text "The screen pass is the row named "}
        {code "voxelgiGiCS"}
        {text ", after its compute shader"}}}}.

${overText {' {qualityRange 1.0 0.0}} "This chapter shows how Godot keeps that row small"}.

${cue "key" {' unhighlight}}These times are this machine's, so on another machine they will differ.

${overText
    {' {giPropagationRange 0.52 0.5}}
    "A row appears only when its pass actually runs, so a missing row means skipped work"}.

### Light passes only on change {#c8-cache kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelPerformance 1.0 {' unhighlight {giRelightEachFrame 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 16.0 6.6} {pose 30.0 20.0 5.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "update-gate"}}}Right now, no light pass is listed.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 36.0 sentence}}}Nothing changed, and this room has no moving object, so Godot does no light-pass work at all.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-cache"} {extraLight 1.0}}}${overText
    {' {lightXRange 0.9 0.2}}
    "Every frame, the culler from chapter four compares each visible light near the box with its saved copy, on the processor"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "relight-gate"}}}${overText
    {' {giDynamicRangeRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "The light passes run only when a light, the baked data, or one of three settings has changed"}.

${overText
    {' {giPropagationRange 0.5 0.8}}
    "Those settings are the dynamic range, the propagation from chapter five, and whether the second bounce is on"}.

${cue "key" {' {giRelightEachFrame 1.0}}}To measure the light passes, the lab now relights the room every frame.

Godot would do that only when something changes.

${cue "key" {'
    {highlightPasses {' "voxelgiLightCS" "voxelgiBounceCS" "voxelgiMipmapsCS" "voxelgiWriteCS"}}}}Four rows appear, for the direct light pass, the bounce pass, the mip pass, and the write pass.

${cue "key" {' {highlightPasses {' "voxelgiBounceCS"}}}}${overText
    {' {giTwoBouncesRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "On this machine, the bounce takes the longest, because it traces six cones from every leaf that has a normal"}.

${cue "key" {' unhighlight}}${overText
    {' {giRelightEachFrameRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "When the lab returns to Godot's behaviour, those rows are gone, so a still room's light costs nothing per frame"}.

### Only the occupied cells {#c8-sparse kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelValues 0.5 {'
    {focusRow -1.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}
    {giRelightEachFrame 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 24.0 6.6} {pose 28.0 30.0 6.0}}}}}}}

Times depend on the machine, so this part counts the work instead.

${cue "key" {' {focusRow 1.0}}}The readout on the right counts what one update of the light passes costs, and what one frame costs.

This chapter's numbers come from it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sort-cells"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 7.0}}
    "When the light passes do run, they visit only the cells the bake made"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "Those are the cells on screen, not the empty grid around them"}.

${over
    {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 1.2}}
    {overText
        {' {focusRowRange 3.0 4.0}}
        {++ {++ "That is " {rounded {leaves} 2}} " leaves, the cells of the finest level"}}}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.2 3.2}}
    "The texture's finest mip has more than a million texels, so the light passes skip almost all of the texture"}.

### A level at a time {#c8-levels kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelValues 0.5 {'
    {focusRow -1.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cutHeight 1.6}
    {giRelightEachFrame 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 22.0 28.0 6.4} {pose 30.0 32.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' insetCellArray imageCornerBottomLeft {callout "sort-cells"}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.6 2.0}}
    "The cells are stored sorted by level, with the root first and the leaves last"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 2.0 2.4}}
    "So each level is one run of the array, drawn as one colour in this bar"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "local-size"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Each run is one dispatch, one call to the graphics card"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "As in chapter four, each workgroup of a dispatch takes sixty-four cells"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "level-dispatch"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 5.0}}
    "The direct light pass comes first, and lights the leaves"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 3.0}}
    "Then the mip pass climbs one level per dispatch, and each dispatch waits for the one before it to finish"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 1.0}}
    "That wait is called a barrier, and it makes sure the children are finished before a parent sums their light"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pass-order"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 1.0 7.0}}
    "Then eight writes, one for each level of the octree, copy the cells into the texture's mips"}.

${overText {' {focusRowRange 2.0 1.0}} "So one round of light takes sixteen dispatches"}.

One lights the leaves, seven build the mips, and eight write the texture.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 1.0}}
    f"{With the second bounce on, a second round repeats them, so one update of this room takes ${spoken {dispatches}} dispatches}"}.

${cue "key" {' {imageHide {' "cellArray"}} {focusRow -1.0}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 1.0 -1.0}}
    "The lab's own light passes run in that same order"}.

### Shadows step by the field {#c8-march kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelHidden 0.3 {'
    {workedLight 1.0}
    {lightStep 4.0}
    {marchSteps 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 0.0 16.0 4.4} {pose 8.0 20.0 4.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "raymarch"}}}${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Inside the direct light pass, each shadow ray from chapter four reads the distance field"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "The field says how far the nearest surface is, so the ray can safely jump to one cell short of it"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 3.0 12.0}}
    "So its steps are long in open air, and short only near a surface"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 12.0 26.0}}
    "A step is never shorter than one cell along the ray's main axis"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 26.0 40.0}}
    "So the march is never slower than a walk from cell to cell"}.

Some lights never need a march at all.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-at-pos"}}}${overText
    {' {lightXRange 0.9 2.8} {lightZRange -0.4 2.8}}
    "A light that is out of range, behind the cell, or dimmed to under one percent is skipped before any march starts"}.

### A few samples per pixel {#c8-cones kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {coneSteps 0.0}
    {specCone 0.0}
    {quality 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 34.0 18.0} {aim 44.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "cones-low"}}}Everything so far runs only on change.

But the screen pass runs every frame, so its price is paid per pixel.

${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 0.0 {float lowCones}}}
    f"{At the default quality, a pixel traces ${spoken lowCones} cones}"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 0.0 {float {lowSteps}}}}
    f"{In this box, each cone takes at most ${spoken {lowSteps}} steps, so a pixel takes at most ${spoken {* lowCones {lowSteps}}} samples}"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "Each sample reads one texel, from the mip whose texels are as wide as the cone at that point"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 5.0 6.0}}
    "That texel already combines the light of the cells it covers, so it stands in cheaply for many rays"}.

${overText {' {coneStepsRange 6.0 7.0}} "The price is that detail thinner than that texel is lost"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cones-high"}}}${over
    {' {qualityRange 0.0 1.0}}
    {over
        {' {conesShownRange {float lowCones} {float highCones}}}
        {overText
            {' {coneStepsRange {float {lowSteps}} {float {highSteps}}}}
            f"{At High, ${spoken highCones} narrower cones widen more slowly}"}}}.

${cue "key" {' panelPerformance {highlightPasses {' "voxelgiGiCS"}}}}So their steps are shorter, and there are more of them.

The screen pass's row grows.

${cue "key" {' {callout "quality-doc"} {specCone 1.0}}}${overText
    {' {qualityRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "Godot's documentation says High also gives better reflections"}.

${overText
    {' {conesShownRange {float highCones} {float lowCones}}}
    "But in the code, High changes only the cones for ambient light"}.

The reflection cone stays the same.

### The reflection cone {#c8-spec kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {'
    demoPixel
    {callout "spec-cone"}
    {inset ""}
    panelValues
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {specCone 1.0}
    {sphereRoughness 1.0}
    {cameraDrift 0.3}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 38.0 16.0} {aim 30.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 1.0 0.5}}
    "That reflection cone is the one to watch, because its width follows the roughness"}.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.5 0.0}}
    f"{In this box, a fully rough surface takes at most ${spoken {roughSteps}} steps, but a mirror takes up to ${spoken {mirrorSteps}}}"}.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.0 0.04}}
    "A mirror's cone is nearly a ray, so it steps by half a cell, the smallest step the code allows"}.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.04 0.07}}
    "Steps that short never skip a cell, so the mirror's cone visits every cell along its path"}.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.07 0.1}}
    "So mirror-like surfaces are the most expensive pixels"}.

### Half resolution {#c8-half kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelPerformance 0.3 {'
    {highlightPasses {' "voxelgiGiCS"}}
    {giHalfResolution 0.0}
    {costSplit 1.0}
    {viewMode 0.0}
    {quality 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.6 0.7 -0.4}}
    {cameraYaw 40.0}
    {cameraPitch 16.0}
    {cameraDistance 3.4}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {'
            {pose 40.0 16.0 3.4}
            {pose 44.0 17.0 3.0}
            {pose 40.0 15.0 1.7}
            {pose 36.0 16.0 1.6}
            {pose 40.0 16.0 3.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-res-setting"}}}${overText
    {' {giHalfResolutionRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "One project setting, half resolution, trades quality for speed"}.

${overText {' {giHalfResolutionRange 1.0 0.0}} "It is off by default"}.

On the left is the frame at full resolution, and on the right the frame with the setting on.

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-res-buffers"}}}${overText
    {' {giHalfResolutionRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The screen pass's two buffers, for ambient light and reflection, become half as wide and half as tall"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-res-shift"}}}Each of their texels traces only the top-left pixel of its two by two block.

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-res-dispatch"}}}So only a quarter of the pixels are traced.

The screen pass's row shrinks with them.

${overText {' {viewModeRange 0.0 1.0}} "Here are the ambient buffers at full and half size"}.

Each pixel shows the cones' light it reads from its buffer, before any albedo multiplies it.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The opaque pass then reads that half-size buffer at full size, in a way that depends on multisampling, an anti-aliasing mode"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "gi-buffer-fetch"}}}${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Without multisampling, Godot reads it with a plain bilinear blend, and the lab always reads it that way"}.

${cue "key" {' {giHalfResolution 0.0}}}That blend does not weight by depth.

${overText
    {' {giHalfResolutionRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "So along the sphere's edge, texels traced on the wall and on the sphere are blended together"}.

The edge turns ragged, in steps two pixels wide.

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-res-doc"}}}The documentation warns of these artifacts on polygon edges.

${cue "key" {' {viewMode 0.0}}}Those two pixels are one half-size texel, so at a higher screen resolution the artifacts shrink.

### Next: into the image {#c8-recap kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {giHalfResolution 0.0}
    {costSplit 0.0}
    {viewMode 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 16.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 26.0 20.0 6.2}}}}}}}

So VoxelGI costs little each frame.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 7.0}}
    "Its light passes run only on change, and only over occupied cells"}.

Each pixel takes a few cone samples.

And with half resolution on, only a quarter of the pixels are traced.

Each saving has its price.

${overText
    {' {giHalfResolutionRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "A moving light must relight the room, coarse cones lose thin detail, and half resolution frays the edges"}.

What is left is to turn the buffers into colour.

### Where to read {#c8-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="update-gate, light-cache, relight-gate, sort-cells, level-dispatch, local-size, pass-order, raymarch, light-at-pos, cones-low, cones-high, quality-doc, spec-cone, half-res-setting, half-res-buffers, half-res-shift, half-res-dispatch, gi-buffer-fetch, half-res-doc"}

These are the lines that keep the cost small, from the check that skips unchanged light to the half-size buffers.

## Into the final image {#c9 number=9 target=195 summary="The opaque pass mixes the environment toward VoxelGI's light by its alpha; one pixel followed back; three takeaways." sources="gi-buffer-mix, forward-gi-transparent, forward-cones, fog-inject, debug-noop, bias-mismatch"}

### The opaque pass {#c9-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="CONE, OPAQUE"}

Now the opaque pass turns the screen pass's two buffers into colour.

One buffer holds the ambient light, and the other holds the reflection.

### Mixed with the environment by alpha {#c9-mix kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoCompose panelValues 0.5 {' {viewMode 0.0} {interior 0.0} {focusRow 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 36.0 14.0} {aim 46.0 18.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "gi-buffer-mix"}}}${over
    {' {viewModeRange 0.0 1.0}}
    {overText {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 90.0}} "The ambient buffer holds the cones' light"}}.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "Its alpha, the coverage, holds how much of the view the cones found filled"}.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 3.0 0.0}}
    "A cone that leaves the box finds nothing, so its alpha stays low"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Where the alpha is low, the opaque pass mixes toward the environment's ambient light, here the sky's"}.

The reflection is mixed with the sky's reflection the same way.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "So the sky still lights what the cones saw as empty, here through the room's open top and front"}.

${overText
    {' {interiorRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "With the interior setting on, the alpha is one, so the sky never mixes in"}.

After that, the mixed light becomes the pixel's ambient light, and the rest of the pass treats it like any other.

${over
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 7.0}}
    {overText
        {' {pixelAzimuthRange 75.0 60.0}}
        "From there, the opaque pass applies ambient occlusion and the albedo to it"}}.

Then it applies screen-space effects and the environment's reflectance.

### Transparent surfaces and fog read it too {#c9-others kind=3d density=0.4}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {voxelgiMix 1.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cellView 4.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 14.0 16.0 6.6} {pose 28.0 20.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-gi-transparent"}}}${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Only opaque surfaces read the screen pass's buffers"}.

This room has no transparent surface, but Godot lights such surfaces another way.

Each transparent surface traces its own four cones, at every quality setting.

It traces them in the forward pass that draws it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "fog-inject"}}}${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "Volumetric fog reads the texture too, with no cone at all"}.

At each point of the fog's grid inside the box, it reads every mip of the texture, so the fog takes in VoxelGI's light at every level of detail.

${cue "key" {' {callout "debug-noop"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 7.0}}
    "Godot's lighting debug view, which draws the cells' light as small cubes, also reads the texture"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 1.0 0.75}}
    "But that view never multiplies the light back by the dynamic range"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.75 0.5}}
    "So at the default range of two, that view shows VoxelGI's light at half the strength the screen pass really adds"}.

### One pixel, followed back {#c9-follow kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelHidden 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 4.0}
    {specCone 0.0}
    {coneSteps 16.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 36.0 16.0 3.2} {pose 24.0 22.0 3.6} {pose 32.0 18.0 3.0}}}}}}}

Follow this pixel on the sphere back through the whole chain.

${overText {' {coneStepsRange 16.0 2.0}} "Its cones read the texture's mips around the pixel"}.

${cue "key" {' demoMips {slice 2.0} {mipLevel 0.0} {workedParent 1.0} panelHidden}}${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "The mip pass built those mips from the leaves"}.

${cue "key" {' demoBounce {workedBounce 1.0} {conesShown 0.0} {coneSteps 6.0} panelHidden}}${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 0.0 6.0}}
    "Those leaves hold the ceiling panel's direct light, plus one bounce"}.

${overText {' {coneStepsRange 1.0 6.0}} "Each leaf gathered that bounce with six cones of its own"}.

${cue "key" {'
    demoOctree
    {workedTriangle 1.0}
    {workedLeaf 1.0}
    {leafColumns 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.01 0.24 -1.71}}
    {cameraDistance 0.4}
    panelHidden}}${over
        {' {leafColumnsRange 0.0 16.0}}
        {overText
            {' {descentLevelRange 5.0 7.0}}
            "And at the very start, the bake averaged samples of the room's triangles into each leaf's albedo, emission, and normal"}}.

### Three takeaways {#c9-recap kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {voxelgiMix 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cellView 4.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 16.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 26.0 20.0 6.2} {pose 20.0 18.0 6.4}}}}}}}

${overText {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}} "The first takeaway is the data"}.

A sparse octree is baked once, then poured into a dense texture with one mip per level.

The second is the light.

Light passes run when the lights or the settings change.

Every frame, every pixel then traces cones that read wider mips as they go.

The third is the trade.

The static scene must be baked.

Moving objects are only drawn in, frame by frame.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 7.0}}
    "The light keeps no detail finer than a cell, five centimetres in this room"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 4.0}}
    "And the cones read coarse averages of those cells, so they lose thin detail"}.

In the light passes, area lights like the ceiling panel cast no shadows.

In return, a pixel needs only a few cone samples.

### Where to read {#c9-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="gi-buffer-mix, forward-gi-transparent, forward-cones, fog-inject, debug-noop, bias-mismatch"}

These are the lines of the opaque pass's mix, and those of the other passes that read the texture.

Every source this video cites is in its source-reading guide, chapter by chapter.
